{"task":{"prompt":"A tripod-mounted camera at 1/500 s captures a cyclist and a wall both sharp. The photographer then handholds at 1/60 s; both the cyclist and the stationary wall appear blurred. A stationary object cannot show subject motion blur. What primarily caused the wall's blur in the second shot?","topic":"Diagnosing motion blur in photography","objective":"Interpret a concrete photographic result and shooting constraints to distinguish subject motion blur from camera movement blur","difficulty":3}}
```

```json
{
  "distractors": [
    {
      "text": "Overexposure from the slower shutter speed washed out and softened the wall's detail",
      "explanation": "Overexposure clips highlights and raises brightness but does not cause the directional smearing of edges characteristic of motion blur. Exposure level and motion blur are distinct phenomena; the wall's blur shows displaced detail, not merely blown-out tones."
    },
    {
      "text": "The wall experienced subtle subject motion blur from air vibrations during the longer exposure",
      "explanation": "A solid wall does not move perceptibly from a passing cyclist's air currents. Subject motion blur requires the subject itself to shift significantly relative to the sensor during exposure. The wall is genuinely stationary, so this mechanism cannot apply."
    },
    {
      "text": "A shallower depth of field in the second shot placed the wall outside the plane of sharp focus",
      "explanation": "Depth of field is governed by aperture, focal length and subject distance—not shutter speed directly. The scenario does not state the aperture changed. Moreover, defocus blur would not depend on whether the camera was tripod-mounted or handheld, yet the tripod shot was sharp."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": "A stationary wall cannot exhibit subject motion blur. The sole change affecting the wall between the two shots was switching from a tripod to handholding at a slower shutter speed. Camera shake during 1/60 s moved the entire image across the sensor, blurring every element—stationary and moving—uniformly. The decisive clue is that the object has no motion of its own, so the blur must originate from the camera's movement.",
  "answerExplanation": "Because the wall is motionless, its blur cannot come from subject motion. Removing the tripod introduced camera shake, and at 1/60 s the exposure lasted long enough for hand movement to shift the image across the sensor, producing blur on all scene elements—including the stationary wall. This matches the source principle that longer durations record more motion; here the relevant motion is the camera's own."
}
```

